<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Halfdan Ragnarsson</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 877 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Halfdan Ragnarsson was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-heathen-army">Great Heathen Army</a> that invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> in 865, later described as a son of Ragnar Lodbrok. With his brother Ivar he took York in 866–867, and he campaigned in Mercia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wessex">Wessex</a> and against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/picts">Picts</a> and Strathclyde. In 876, according to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxon-chronicle">Anglo-Saxon Chronicle</a>, he shared out the land of Northumbria among his followers, who began to farm it.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Halfdan Ragnarsson, in Old English Halfdene or Healfdene and in Old Irish Albann, was one of six sons of Ragnar Lodbrok named in the Norse sagas. His brothers and half-brothers included Björn Ironside, Ivar the Boneless, Sigurd Snake-in-the-Eye, Ubba and Hvitserk. Because Halfdan appears in no source that mentions Hvitserk, some scholars suggest they were one man, Hvitserk, 'white shirt', being a nickname that set him apart from other men called Halfdan. Asser and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxon-chronicle">Anglo-Saxon Chronicle</a> name Halfdan and Ivar as brothers.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Halfdan led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-heathen-army">Great Heathen Army</a> that landed in East Anglia in the autumn of 865. According to the sagas, the sons of Ragnar organised it to avenge him on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aella-of-northumbria">Ælla of Northumbria</a>, who had supposedly had him thrown into a snake pit. The army invaded Northumbria, then in civil war between Ælla and Osberht, and conquered York late in 866. Ælla and Osberht both fell in a failed attempt to retake it, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danes">Danes</a> installed a puppet king, Ecgberht.</p><p>The army then took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nottingham">Nottingham</a> in Mercia and withdrew to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/york">York</a> under a truce. After the conquest of East Anglia, where Ivar and Ubba are named as commanders, Halfdan became the main commander and led an invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wessex">Wessex</a> in 870. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danes">Danes</a> fought the West Saxons nine times, including at Ashdown on 8 January 871, and Halfdan accepted a truce from Alfred. Coins minted in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in the following winter bear his name.</p><p>The army conquered Mercia in 874, deposing King Burghred and installing the puppet regent Ceolwulf. It then split, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guthrum">Guthrum</a> going south against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wessex">Wessex</a> and Halfdan north against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/picts">Picts</a> and the Britons of Strathclyde. He is generally agreed to be the Albann who, according to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/annals-of-ulster">Annals of Ulster</a>, killed Eystein Olafsson, King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a>, in 875. Halfdan then sailed up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-tyne">River Tyne</a>, sacked the monasteries at Tynemouth and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jarrow">Jarrow</a>, and camped at Gateshead. His men settled an area largely coextensive with the old Kingdom of Deira, and sources sometimes title him King of Jórvík.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Halfdan was deposed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a> while away in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/york">York</a>. In 877 he returned to Ireland to recapture the city but met an army of Fair Heathens, usually taken to be the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> population longest in Ireland, and was killed at the Battle of Strangford Lough. His surviving men returned to Northumbria through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, fighting a battle in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-i">Constantine I</a>, King of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/picts">Picts</a>, was killed. The Vikings of Northumbria had no king until Guthred was made king in 883.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Halfdan Ragnarsson” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Vikings set up dependent English kings north of the Tyne, and in 876 their leader <b>Halfdan</b> shared out the lands of southern Northumbria among his followers, founding the Viking Kingdom of York.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-northumbria">Kingdom of Northumbria · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The Great Heathen Army captured York in 866 to 867, and in 876 <b>Halfdan</b> shared out the land of Northumbria south of the Tees among his followers.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-york">Kingdom of York</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scandinavia">Scandinavia</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/sweden">Sweden</a>. Died in Strangford Lough, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=64.78,14.76&amp;year=867" aria-label="Scandinavia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Halfdan Ragnarsson"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5532 4532h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5532 4532h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=64.78,14.76&amp;year=867">What was here in 867 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
